[/asy] Because triangles $AED$ and $BEC$ are isosceles, we have $\angle AED=\theta,$ and $\angle EBC = 90^\circ-\frac{(\angle EBC+\theta)}{2}.$ Since $\angle AED = 90^\circ-x,$ it follows that $\frac{\theta(x+\theta)}{2}=\frac{(3\theta-x)}{2},$ which yields $\theta=3x.$ Thus $\angle EBC = 3\theta - x = 3(2x)-x=5x,$ and by the law of (sine) sines, we have $\frac{x}{\sin\theta}=\frac{x}{\sin\theta},$ then $x=\frac{\theta}{2}\rightarrow\theta=3x=60^\circ,$ and $\angle EBC=5\theta=\boxed{150}$ degrees. </think> <answer> 150 </answer>

