 We try to construct an octagon with as many interior right angles as possible, while maintaining the requirement that it is a convex shape. There are 360 degree rotations. If we distribute three such rotations equally on each exterior angle, we will have at least 4 right angles (since we have four $360^\circ/4=90^\circ$ rotations). We have 240 degree rotations left, we will put back on each exterior angle from the original octagon, so we now have 8 right angles, and the other 6 angles will be acute because the other angles in the octagon are $\angle A_1\cdots\angle A_6$. Since all angles in the octagon are less than $180^\circ$, the octagon is convex. We end up with 8 right angles, and we can't have more than 8 because there are only 360 degrees of rotation. We see this confirms our insight: an octagon can have 3 acute angles and 5 right angles, by replacing its base angles $A_1,A_5$ and its tip angles $A_4,A_6$ with small equilateral triangles.

